{"title":"Cobalt Museums","sub":"Raise income without turning visitors away","type":"Pricing","industry":"Arts and tourism","geo":"Italy","diff":2,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Peak pricing · public sector objectives","prompt":"Cobalt manages three city-owned museums in Bologna, with 900,000 paying visitors a year at a single $18 ticket (free first-Sunday visits for residents are extra). The city wants more income to fund restoration, but the museums' mission is broad public access, and weekend queues are already long. The director asks you to design a better price structure.","clarify":[{"q":"When do people visit?","a":"60% of visits are at peak times (weekends, holidays); 40% off-peak (weekday mornings)."},{"q":"Constraints?","a":"Total visitor numbers must not fall. Free first Sundays for residents must stay."},{"q":"Costs per visitor?","a":"About $3: ticketing, cleaning, guides."},{"q":"Proposal?","a":"Peak tickets at $24, off-peak at $14, booked in time slots."}],"structure":{"root":"Can a new price structure raise income and keep access?","branches":[{"head":"Income","items":["Visitors by time slot","Price by time slot"]},{"head":"Visitor response","items":["Peak visitors who drop out","Off-peak visitors gained (shifted or new)"]},{"head":"Mission","items":["Total visitors","Access for residents and low-income groups","Peak crowding"]}]},"hypothesis":"Peak visitors are mostly tourists with fixed plans, so they're less price-sensitive; cheaper weekday tickets can pull locals and students in. Income could rise and crowding fall at the same time.","questions":[{"q":"Research suggests peak visits fall 8% at $24 and off-peak visits rise 15% at $14. Calculate visitors and contribution, before and after.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"400.538pt\" height=\"185.46pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 400.538 185.46\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{strok